  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Cool, wet weather with winter storm along the Continental Divide\n  continues through the weekend. \n\n- Hard freeze in valleys of western Montana early this week.\n\n- Warming up to above average conditions in the long term.\n\nThe upper level low over the Pacific northwest continues to pump\nmoisture up from the south where it interacts with a cold air mass\nfrom the north today. The main area where the two air masses\ncollide is along the Continental Divide, and there's a broad area\nof winter storm warnings and winter weather advisories many miles\neither side of the Continental Divide as a result. Overnight snow\nstarted accumulating on roadways in the mountains and even some\nlower elevation areas like Garrison Junction. The further west,\nthe weaker the interaction and the less moisture to work with, and\nin the valleys of central Idaho there has been little to no impact\nfrom this system. \n\nEast winds along the Continental Divide are also causing impacts.\nAs the cold air spills over the mountains and accelerates down\nhill it has been causing frequent gusts around 40 mph on the east\nshores of Flathead Lake and Badrock Canyon. Those conditions are\nexpected to continue today, but to a lesser extent as the valleys\nof western Montana start to fill up with colder air. \n\nMuch colder air will be in the place over the region once the\nupper level low moves out of the region early this week. As a\nresult, many locations in western Montana will cool into the low\n20s and a few of the typically colder spots down into the teens\nMonday through Wednesday mornings. In central Idaho, conditions\nwill be more mild with valleys cooling into the 30s and low 40s. \n\nEnsembles still agree well on a ridge building in quickly mid to\nlate next week, and as a result by the end of the week afternoon\ntemperatures will be above average again. However, due to a drier\nair mass in place at that time and longer nights, we do expect\ncold mornings to continue through the week.",
